     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.914,
      "literature": 0.086
     },
     "said": [
      "To analyze local extinction risk, we partitioned all moth species under consideration into three groups: species observed only before 1997 (named ‘lost’ hereafter), species recorded only after 1997 (named ‘previously unrecorded’), and species occurring before and after 1997 (named ‘persistent’).",
      "## Results In PsV, we counted 63 lost, 156 persistent, and 81 previously unrecorded moth species, summing up to a total of 300 species ([Supp Table 1](http://academic.oup.com/jinsectscience/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/jisesa/ieaa097#supplementary-data) [online only])."
     ],
     "value": "measured"
    },
    "Persistent": {
     "choice": "measured",
     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.974,
      "literature": 0.026
     },
     "said": [
      "To analyze local extinction risk, we partitioned all moth species under consideration into three groups: species observed only before 1997 (named ‘lost’ hereafter), species recorded only after 1997 (named ‘previously unrecorded’), and species occurring before and after 1997 (named ‘persistent’).",
      "Our results show that locally extinct species (mean wingspan: 37.0 mm) were larger than persistent (33.2 mm) or previously unrecorded ones (30.7 mm), leading to a smaller mean wingspan of the moth community over time."
     ],
     "value": "measured"
    },
